      <h1>Tech Market Sell-Off: AI Capex Concerns and Geopolitical Tensions</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Global technology markets face a significant risk-off rotation as AI spending fatigue and crude oil spikes trigger widespread investor anxiety.</p>
    </header>

    <article>
      <p>The global technology market has experienced a significant risk-off sell-off, driven by escalating concerns over massive AI capital expenditure (capex) and rising geopolitical tensions.</p>
      
      <p>The downturn triggered an intense rotation away from large-cap artificial intelligence plays. While major benchmarks fell heavily, market breadth under the surface actually remained positive, indicating that the damage was heavily concentrated within crowded AI and semiconductor trades rather than the broader economy.</p>

      <h2>Key Market Movements</h2>
      
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nasdaq Composite:</strong> Suffered a 2.6% plunge, marking its worst single-day drop in more than four months.</li>
        <li><strong>S&P 500:</strong> Dropped 1.2%, heavily weighed down by its tech components, even though more than half of the stocks in the index finished higher.</li>
        <li><strong>Dow Jones Industrial Average:</strong> Remained roughly flat due to its minimal exposure to the tech sector.</li>
        <li><strong>Safe Havens:</strong> U.S. Treasuries rallied aggressively, causing the 10-year yield to drop below 5%, while the Japanese Yen climbed to a one-month high.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Primary Catalysts Behind the Rout</h2>
      
      <p><strong>The AI Spend Fatigue:</strong> Meta Platforms ignited intense investor anxiety after reporting earnings that fell short of net profit expectations while simultaneously raising its future capital spending forecast to $135bn – $145bn. This sparked fears that tech giants (hyperscalers) are over-borrowing and over-spending on AI infrastructure without generating immediate, matching returns.</p>
      
      <p><strong>Geopolitical Crude Spike:</strong> Brent crude futures surged above $114 a barrel (the highest level since May) following renewed strikes on Saudi Arabia and rising conflicts involving the US, Israel, and Iran. Higher energy prices stoked renewed fears of sticky inflation and high borrowing costs.</p>
      
      <p><strong>Hedge Fund Deleveraging:</strong> The sudden decline in crowded trades forced quantitative and long-short hedge funds into a rapid risk-reduction phase, causing tech-focused funds to drop roughly 10% in October.</p>

